* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files need CRLF line endings to run reliably. `-text` turns
# off line-ending conversion for these files, so a checkout writes the blob's
# bytes unchanged, and the CRLF must be in the blob itself. To show it, run
# this command in a clone. It stores an LF-only blob and converts it as a
# checkout of start_windows.bat would:
#   printf 'a\nb\n' | git hash-object -w --stdin | xargs git cat-file --filters --path=start_windows.bat | od -c
# Under `-text` the output shows `a \n b \n`, with no conversion. Under
# `text eol=crlf` it shows a `\r` before each `\n`.
# scripts/tests/test_launcher_line_endings.sh checks that each blob is CRLF.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text

# Shell/command launchers must stay LF
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
